#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""tools/engine-forwarders -- engine interfaces through generated native forwarders.

    tools/engine-forwarders               regenerate and DIFF against what is committed
    tools/engine-forwarders --write       write the generated files
    tools/engine-forwarders --write --reorder
                                          write them even where a table's slots
                                          are not the committed ones plus new
                                          ones at the end (rebuild both sides)
    tools/engine-forwarders --self-test   check this script's own refusals

    exit 0   the committed files are what the headers say
    exit 1   they are not -- run --write and read the diff
    exit 2   a header could not be read, or a forwarded method is not in it

plan_engine_forwarders.md. Managed used to call an engine interface by indexing
the engine object's vtable through a struct in VtableLayouts.g.cs, and so had to
know both C++ ABIs: MSVC gives a virtual destructor one slot rather than two and
lays each class's overloads out grouped and in reverse declaration order. A
forwarder takes that away. For every method managed calls this writes one line
of C++,

    static const model_t *SSFwd_ModelInfo_GetModel( IVModelInfo *self, int a0 )
        { return self->GetModel( a0 ); }

and the compiler, which knows the layout of the ABI it builds for, does the
dispatch. `self->GetModel` is still a virtual call through the engine object's
own vtable, so whatever the engine's class overrides is what runs.

ONE TABLE PER INTERFACE, IN A FIXED ORDER. The forwarders of one interface are
an array of plain function pointers, in the order FORWARD below names them,
served through SSForwarderTable (src/fwd/native/ss_fwd.h): the array and its
count. A plain array has one layout on every ABI, so the C# struct generated
beside it is a struct of plain fields, `delegate* unmanaged<...>`, and a call is
`_fwd->GetModel(_self, index)`: the same shape as the vtable call it replaces.

SLOTS ONLY EVER APPEND, SO THE CHECK AT BIND IS A COUNT. A method joins its
table at the end of its FORWARD row, never in header order, and --write refuses
a table whose committed slots are not, name and signature, the first slots of
the new one. So a table only grows at its end, and an assembly built against
an older table reads a prefix of a newer one. Forwarders.TryAcquire holds only
that native serves at least as many slots as the struct has fields. This is
where the guarantee lives: the check at bind has nothing to hold against a
slot that moved, so a slot never moves. `--write --reorder` lifts the refusal
for a deliberate break, after which only a rebuild of both sides is safe.

WHAT IS FORWARDED IS WHAT MANAGED CALLS, not every slot: a method enters
FORWARD when managed code first calls it. The names are the field names
VtableLayouts.g.cs gives (an overload's second declaration is `Name_2`), so the
two files can be read side by side while both exist.

THE OUTPUTS
    src/fwd/native/fwd_engine.g.inc   the forwarders and their tables, included by
                                      src/fwd/native/fwd_engine.cpp
    src/abi/EngineForwarders.g.cs     one struct per table: its name, its slot
                                      hashes and names, one field per forwarder
"""

